{"items":[{"id":"cap-v2-ordering_and_selection-typescript-generation-01","problem":"Implement a complete TypeScript source file exporting exactly the following declarations:

```ts
export interface ReviewCandidate {
  id: string;
  priority: number;
  quality: number;
  arrival: number;
  labels: string[];
}

export function selectReviewCandidates(
  candidates: ReviewCandidate[],
  limit: number,
): ReviewCandidate[];
```

The arguments satisfy these contracts: `limit` is an integer from `0` through `candidates.length`, all numeric fields are finite, and candidate IDs are unique.

Rank candidates by higher `priority`, then higher `quality`, then lower `arrival`, and finally lexicographically smaller `id` using UTF-16 code-unit order. Return the first `limit` candidates in that order.

The function must not mutate `candidates`, its objects, or their `labels` arrays. Every returned candidate object and every returned `labels` array must be newly allocated, so mutations to the result cannot affect the input.

Use only the standard language and runtime APIs. Do not access the filesystem, network, clock, or randomness.","reference_code":"export interface ReviewCandidate {
  id: string;
  priority: number;
  quality: number;
  arrival: number;
  labels: string[];
}

function compareText(left: string, right: string): number {
  return left < right ? -1 : left > right ? 1 : 0;
}

export function selectReviewCandidates(
  candidates: ReviewCandidate[],
  limit: number,
): ReviewCandidate[] {
  const ranked = [...candidates].sort((left, right) => {
    if (left.priority !== right.priority) {
      return right.priority - left.priority;
    }
    if (left.quality !== right.quality) {
      return right.quality - left.quality;
    }
    if (left.arrival !== right.arrival) {
      return left.arrival - right.arrival;
    }
    return compareText(left.id, right.id);
  });

  return ranked.slice(0, limit).map((candidate) => ({
    ...candidate,
    labels: [...candidate.labels],
  }));
}
