ssRuns<T>(\\n    items: readonly T[],\\n    same: (left: T, right: T) => boolean,\\n): Run<T>[];\\n```\\n\\nScan `items` from left to right and replace each maximal consecutive run of equivalent items with one `Run`. Use `same` to compare an item only with the representative value of the current run. Store the first item of a run as its `value` and the run length as `count`.\\n\\nEquivalent values separated by a non-equivalent item belong to different runs. For example, an `A, B, A` sequence produces three runs even when the two `A` values are equivalent. An empty input produces an empty array.\\n\\nThe implementation must be deterministic and use no filesystem, network, clock, randomness, external package, or framework.\",\"reference_code\":\"export interface Run<T> {\\n    value: T;\\n    count: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport function compressRuns<T>(\\n    items: readonly T[],\\n    same: (left: T, right: T) => boolean,\\n): Run<T>[] {\\n    const runs: Run<T>[] = [];\\n\\n    for (const item of items) {\\n        const current = runs[runs.length - 1];\\n        if (current !== undefined && same(current.value, item)) {\\n            current.count += 1;\\n        } else {\\n            runs.push({ value: item, count: 1 });\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    return runs;\\n}\\n\",\"known_bad_code\":\"export interface Run<T> {\\n    value: T;\\n    count: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport function compressRuns<T>(\\n    items: readonly T[],\\n    same: (left: T, right: T) => boolean,\\n): Run<T>[] {\\n    const runs: Run<T>[] = [];\\n\\n    for (const item of items) {\\n        const existing = runs.find(run => same(run.value, item));\\n        if (existing !== undefined) {\\n            existing.count += 1;\\n        } else {\\n            runs.push({ value: item, count: 1 });\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    return runs;\\n}\\n\",\"known_bad_label\":\"Combines all equivalent values globally instead of forming distinct maximal consecutive runs.\"},{\"id\":\"cap-v2-sequence_algorithms-typescript-generation-02\",\"problem\":\"Implement a duplicate-suppressing weave of two ordered sequences.\\n\\nExport exactly this API:\\n\\n```typescript\\nexport function weaveUnique<T>(\\n    first: readonly T[],\\n    second: readonly T[],\\n    keyOf: (item: T) => string,\\n): T[];\\n```\\n\\nProcess the inputs in index rounds. At each index `i`, consider `first[i]` if present and then `second[i]` if present. Append a considered item only when no previously appended item from either input has the same `keyOf` result. Thus, the first encountered item for a key wins, including when duplicates occur within one input or across both inputs. Continue until both sequences are exhausted and return the emitted items in encounter order.\\n\\nThe implementation must be deterministic and use no filesystem, network, clock, randomness, external package, or framework.\",\"reference_code\":\"export function weaveUnique<T>(\\n    first: readonly T[],\\n    second: readonly T[],\\n    keyOf: (item: T) => string,\\n): T[] {\\n    const result: T[] = [];\\n    const seen = new Set<string>();\\n    const rounds = Math.max(first.length, second.length);\\n\\n    for (let index = 0; index < rounds; index += 1) {\\n        if (index < first.length) {\\n            const item = first[index];\\n            const key = keyOf(item);\\n            if (!seen.has(key)) {\\n                seen.add(key);\\n                result.push(item);\\n            }\\n        }\\n\\n        if (index < second.length) {\\n            const item = second[index];\\n            const key = keyOf(item);\\n            if (!seen.has(key)) {\\n                seen.add(key);\\n                result.push(item);\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    return result;\\n}\\n\",\"known_bad_code\":\"export function weaveUnique<T>(\\n    first: readonly T[],\\n    second: readonly T[],\\n    keyOf: (item: T) => string,\\n): T[] {\\n    const result: T[] = [];\\n    const seenFirst = new Set<string>();\\n    const seenS